# What Rainbow Scripts Reveal About Old vs New Luau

 
 

 
If you've ever searched "how to make a rainbow part roblox," you've probably

 
noticed something annoying: every script you find does it slightly

 
differently, and none of the tutorials explain why theirs is different from

 
the last one you tried. You copy one, it works, you move on — but if you

 
open two of these scripts side by side, they rarely look like the same

 
language.

 
 

 
That's what this is about. Not "here's how to make a rainbow part" —

 
you'll have that in the first thirty seconds — but "here's what you can

 
tell about a script, and about whoever wrote it, just from *how* it does

 
something whose result never changes."

 
 

 
## Why a rainbow part is a strangely good specimen

 
 

 
Here's the thing that makes rainbow scripts interesting instead of just

 
repetitive: **the visual output is basically the same no matter which

 
version you use.** A part that cycles through hue looks like a part that

 
cycles through hue. Nobody is forced to use one clock function over another

 
to make the color change — both produce an identical-looking rainbow.

 
 

 
Which means every difference in the code is a *pure style choice*. Not a

 
requirement. Not a bug (usually). Just... whichever way someone learned to

 
do it.

 
 

 
That's rare. Most code differs because the *problem* differs — different

 
data, different constraints, different edge cases. A rainbow script has

 
none of that. It's ten lines, no dependencies, no game-specific logic. So

 
when two rainbow scripts look different, you're not looking at two

 
different solutions to two different problems. You're looking at two

 
different eras of the same language, sitting side by side.

 
 

 
I collected 22 real rainbow-part scripts to see if this idea actually held

 
up. It did — and better than I expected. Let's look at why.

 
 

 
---

 
 

 
## The fastest way in: two nearly-identical pairs

 
 

 
Instead of walking through 22 scripts (nobody wants that, including me),

 
let's start with two pairs that are almost line-for-line the same script,

 
except for one deliberate difference each. These two comparisons alone

 
will teach you most of what you need to read *any* Luau script's age.

 
 

 
### Pair 1: the same bug, fixed

 
 

 
```lua

 
-- VERSION A

 
local r, g, b = 255, 0, 0

 
local SPEED = 1

 
 

 
while task.wait() do

 
	if r > 0 and b == 0 then

 
		r -= SPEED

 
		g += SPEED

 
	elseif g > 0 and r == 0 then

 
		g -= SPEED

 
		b += SPEED

 
	elseif b > 0 and g == 0 then

 
		b -= SPEED

 
		r += SPEED

 
	end

 
 

 
	Part.Color = Color3.fromRGB(r, g, b)

 
end

 
```

 
 

 
```lua

 
-- VERSION B

 
local r, g, b = 255, 0, 0

 
local SPEED = 300

 
 

 
game:GetService("RunService").Heartbeat:Connect(function(dt)

 
	local amount = SPEED * dt

 
 

 
	if r > 0 and b == 0 then

 
		r = math.max(0, r - amount)

 
		g = math.min(255, g + amount)

 
	elseif g > 0 and r == 0 then

 
		g = math.max(0, g - amount)

 
		b = math.min(255, b + amount)

 
	elseif b > 0 and g == 0 then

 
		b = math.max(0, b - amount)

 
		r = math.min(255, r + amount)

 
	end

 
 

 
	Part.Color = Color3.new(r / 255, g / 255, b / 255)

 
end)

 
```

 
 

 
Same idea, both times: chase two of the three RGB channels toward each

 
other, one increasing while the other decreases, cycling red → yellow →

 
green → cyan → blue → magenta → red. If you squint, it's the same script.

 
 

 
Now look at the one thing that actually changed: in Version A, `SPEED` is

 
subtracted **once per loop iteration**. In Version B, it's multiplied by

 
`dt` — the time elapsed since the last frame — before being applied.

 
 

 
This sounds like a minor detail. It isn't. Version A's animation speed is

 
secretly coupled to *how often the loop happens to run*. On a smooth 60fps

 
server, that might be roughly what the author intended. On a laggy server,

 
or a device running at 20fps, the same script animates at a fraction of

 
the speed — because it's not measuring time at all, it's just counting

 
iterations and pretending they're evenly spaced.

 
 

 
Version B fixes this by scaling movement by *actual elapsed time*

 
(`dt`), so one second of real time always produces the same amount of color

 
change, regardless of how fast or slow the frames are ticking by.

 
 

 
This is, genuinely, one of the most common invisible bugs in animation

 
code — not just in Roblox, not just in rainbows. Any time you see a loop

 
that moves something by a fixed amount *per iteration* instead of *per

 
second*, you're looking at code that assumes a stable frame rate. That

 
assumption used to be a lot more common than it is now — which is exactly

 
why this pair reads like a before-and-after.

 
 

 
### Pair 2: the same formula, one clock swapped

 
 

 
```lua

 
-- VERSION A

 
while task.wait() do

 
	Part.Color = Color3.fromHSV(tick() % 5 / 5, 1, 1)

 
end

 
```

 
 

 
```lua

 
-- VERSION B

 
local CycleDuration = 5

 
 

 
game:GetService("RunService").Heartbeat:Connect(function()

 
	local hue = (os.clock() % CycleDuration) / CycleDuration

 
	Part.Color = Color3.fromHSV(hue, 1, 1)

 
end)

 
```

 
 

 
Strip away the loop-mechanism difference (we'll get to that) and the core

 
formula is identical: take some clock value, wrap it into a 5-second cycle,

 
divide down to a 0–1 hue. The only real swap is `tick()` for `os.clock()`.

 
 

 
Unlike the first pair, this one isn't a bug fix — both versions work fine,

 
forever, with no visible problem. It's a *convention* shift, not a

 
correction.

 
 

 
Here's the difference: `tick()` returns the number of seconds since the

 
Unix epoch (January 1, 1970) — a huge, constantly growing number, currently

 
in the billions. `os.clock()` returns seconds since some near-zero

 
reference point, usually close to when the script started running.

 
 

 
Why does that matter if both just get wrapped with `%`? Floating-point

 
precision. A `tick()` value like `1798230000.4471` has to spend most of

 
its available precision representing the giant whole-number part, leaving

 
less precision for the fractional part — the part that actually determines

 
your hue. `os.clock()` starts near zero, so almost all its precision is

 
available for the fraction that matters. In practice this rarely causes a

 
*visible* glitch in a 5-second rainbow loop, but it's exactly the kind of

 
thing that becomes a real, hard-to-diagnose problem in anything longer-running

 
or more precision-sensitive — which is part of why `os.clock()` became the

 
recommended convention over time.

 
 

 
So: Pair 1 is *wrong, then fixed*. Pair 2 is *old, then modern*. Different

 
kinds of "difference," and worth being able to tell apart.

 
 

 
---

 
 

 
## Okay, but is this really the whole language, or just one script?

 
 

 
Fair question — one bug-fix pair and one convention-swap pair isn't much to

 
build a whole theory on. So here's where the other 20 scripts earn their

 
keep: sorted by what actually varies between them, they collapse into just

 
a few real decisions being made over and over, wearing different costumes.

 
 

 
### Axis 1 — what drives the animation

 
 

 
| Time source | Scripts using it | What it actually is |

 
|---|---|---|

 
| No real clock — manual counter | most `for`-loop and hand-stepped versions | Just counting iterations, pretending they're evenly spaced in time |

 
| `tick()` | the "old convention" versions | Seconds since the Unix epoch — huge, ever-growing |

 
| `os.clock()` | the "modern convention" versions | Seconds since a near-zero reference point |

 
| `time()` | one version | Roblox's own wrapper — worth knowing it exists as a third option, distinct from both of the above |

 
| `RunService.Heartbeat`'s `dt` | the frame-independent versions | Elapsed seconds *since the last frame*, accumulated manually |

 
| `workspace.DistributedGameTime` | one version | Not a personal clock at all — this is replicated *simulation* time, relevant when multiple clients need to visually agree on what time it is |

 
 

 
### Axis 2 — how the loop itself is driven

 
 

 
- `while true do ... task.wait() end`

 
- `while task.wait() do ... end` (the condition doubles as the loop)

 
- `RunService.Heartbeat:Connect(function(dt) ... end)`

 
- `TweenService`, with `task.wait()` used only for pacing between tweens

 


 
Once you separate these two axes, something clicks: **most of the 22

 
scripts aren't 22 different ideas.** They're 2 or 3 ideas, each expressed

 
through a different combination of "which clock" and "which loop." A

 
handful of them — the ones using `os.clock()` *and* `Heartbeat` *and*

 
`Color3.fromHSV` — are essentially the same script four times over, just

 
with different color math bolted on afterward (more on that in a second).

 
 

 
If you remember nothing else from this section: **when you open an

 
unfamiliar script, check the clock source and the loop mechanism first.**

 
Those two choices, more than anything else in the file, tell you when the

 
author probably learned this pattern.

 
 

 
---

 
 

 
## A quick detour: the color math is a red herring

 
 

 
It's tempting to assume "fancier color math = newer script," but that

 
doesn't hold up in the corpus at all. Several scripts use modern,

 
frame-independent `os.clock()` + `Heartbeat` plumbing, and then differ only

 
in *how* they turn a 0–1 hue into RGB:

 
 

 
- `Color3.fromHSV(hue, 1, 1)` — just calling the built-in

 
- A hand-rolled six-branch HSV conversion (the textbook Wikipedia-style

 
  algorithm, `if h < 1 ... elseif h < 2 ...`)

 
- A "sinebow" — three phase-shifted, squared sine waves, chosen for

 
  smoother, more evenly-weighted color transitions

 
- Raw sine-wave RGB with no HSV concept at all

 


 
All four can sit on top of *identical* clock-and-loop plumbing. So color

 
math tells you about someone's taste, or how deep they went down a

 
different rabbit hole (arguably a more interesting one — see: color theory,

 
perceptual brightness, GPU-friendliness) — but it doesn't reliably tell you

 
when the script was written. Don't let it distract you from the two axes

 
that actually date a script.

 
 

 
